    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "• Current work at the Colorado School of Mines investigates an anaerobic baffled reactor (ABR) for the treatment of raw domestic wastewater at pilot scale.\r\n• The ABR consists of three equally-sized upflow anaerobic sludge blanket compartments followed by a smaller anaerobic fixed film reactor (AFFR) (Fig 1).\r\n• The ABR has continuously treated raw domestic wastewater for over 3 years.\r\n• Effluent quality exceeded EPA secondary standards of 30mg/L for Total Suspended Solids (TSS), even at temperatures below 14 ̊C.\r\n• The ABR can remove over 80% of Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD), converting a majority of influent organic matter to methane and carbon dioxide, but it does not address nutrient removal.\r\n• Integrating partial-nitritation with fixed-film anaerobic ammonium oxidation (anammox) in a single-stage reactor (PN/A) will reduce cost and energy consumption for nitrogen removal, relative to conventional methods.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
